# TypeScript style

Formatting is not your job: `vp fmt` (oxfmt) owns it, configured once in the root
`vite.config.ts` — 100 columns, single quotes, no semicolons, sorted imports, sorted
`package.json`. Write code in the shape below and let the formatter place it.

## Declarations

- Module-level functions are `function` declarations, not an anonymous function bound to a
  `const`. Enforced by `custom/function-declaration`. The exception the rule allows is a `const`
  that carries a type annotation, because the annotation supplies a contextual type.
- A function whose every `return` yields a boolean is a predicate and must be named `isX`,
  `hasX` or `canX`. Enforced by `custom/predicate-is-name`.
- Types are `type` aliases in PascalCase, never `interface`
  (`typescript/consistent-type-definitions: 'type'`, `custom/type-pascal-case`).
- No `let` outside a `for` head (`custom/no-let`). Express the value as one `const` — a ternary
  over the deciding condition, or an extracted function.
- Do not write return types. Let inference do it. Annotate only when a recursive function would
  otherwise resolve to `any`, and then leave the one-line comment the existing code uses:
  `// Recursive: without the annotation the return type resolves to \`any\`.`

## Types

- No `any` and no type assertions in `src`. `assertionStyle` is `never`, so `x as T` and
  `<T>x` both fail; `satisfies` and a real type guard are the way through.
- No non-null assertions (`x!`). `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` is on, so an index access is
  `T | undefined` and you must narrow it.
- Object and array types in the public surface are `readonly`. The OpenAPI model in
  `src/openapi` is entirely readonly and that is deliberate.
- `import type` for type-only imports (`typescript/consistent-type-imports`), and type-only
  specifiers stay in their own `import type` statement.

## Values and control flow

- `const` everywhere, object spread over mutation, `Object.hasOwn` over `hasOwnProperty`.
- Guard clauses over nesting. `curly` is `multi-line`, so `if (!x) return null` on one line is
  legal and a wrapped body must be braced.
- Prefer `??` to `||` for defaults (`typescript/prefer-nullish-coalescing`), `?.` for chained
  access, template literals over concatenation.
- `switch` over a union must be exhaustive (`typescript/switch-exhaustiveness-check`).
- No `console` in `packages/valibot-to-openapi` (it is a library). `test` and `example` are
  scripts and print on purpose, so `no-console` is off there only.

## Imports and module graph

- ESM only. Relative specifiers **always** carry the `.js` extension
  (`import { foo } from '../utils/index.js'`), which NodeNext requires at runtime and `tsc`
  does not check for you. In `test` / `example`, source files import each other with `.ts`.
- No default exports anywhere except a Vite config and the oxlint plugin, each of which already
  carries a scoped `oxlint-disable-next-line import/no-default-export` with its reason.
- No `require`, no CommonJS, no import cycles (`import/no-cycle`).
- No `node:` imports in `packages/valibot-to-openapi` — the library must run in a browser or a
  worker. `node/no-top-level-await` guards the same boundary. `scripts`, `test` and `example`
  are Node programs and may import `node:*`.

## Comments

- Doc comments are TSDoc and describe *why*, never the type — the signature is the type. Do not
  add `@param`/`@returns` boilerplate that restates the signature; a one-line
  `/** JSON pointer to a component schema. */` is the house style. `@example` is welcome on a
  public function.
- No `TODO` / `FIXME` comments (`no-warning-comments`). Parked work belongs in an issue.
- A lint exception lives next to the code as
  `// oxlint-disable-next-line <rule> -- <why>`, never as `'off'` in the config. It must name
  the single rule it silences and state the reason.
